Here's the equivalent C++ code for the provided Python function. This function filters bounding boxes based on their area, similar to the `np.argwhere` and area calculation in NumPy.

We'll use `std::vector` and `std::array` to represent the bounding boxes and their areas. The function will return the indices of the selected boxes.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <array>
#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>

// Assuming the class is part of a larger class, we'll define the function as a member function
// of a class that has a member variable _area_range of type std::pair<double, double>

class BoundingBoxFilter {
public:
    std::pair<double, double> _area_range;

    std::vector<size_t> _select_boxes_by_area(const std::vector<std::array<double, 4>>& boxes_xyxy) {
        if (_area_range.first == 0.0 && _area_range.second == 0.0) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Area range must be specified to filter boxes by area");
        }

        std::vector<size_t> selected_indices;

        for (size_t i = 0; i < boxes_xyxy.size(); ++i) {
            double width = boxes_xyxy[i][2] - boxes_xyxy[i][0];
            double height = boxes_xyxy[i][3] - boxes_xyxy[i][1];
            double area = width * height;

            if (area >= _area_range.first && area <= _area_range.second) {
                selected_indices.push_back(i);
            }
        }

        return selected_indices;
    }
};
```

### Notes:
- The input `boxes_xyxy` is a vector of `std::array<double, 4>`, representing the coordinates of the bounding boxes in the format `[x1, y1, x2, y2]`.
- The function returns a `std::vector<size_t>` containing the indices of the selected boxes.
- The `_area_range` is a `std::pair<double, double>` that defines the minimum and maximum area for selection.
- This version assumes the class is part of a larger class structure. If you're not using a class, you can convert this into a standalone function.

